            "metadata": [
              {
                "app_version": "v0.74.0",
                "chart": "kube-prometheus-stack",
                "first_deployed": 1752787227,
                "last_deployed": 1752787227,
                "name": "kube-prometheus-stack",
                "namespace": "monitoring",
                "notes": "1. Get the application URL by running these commands:\n  export POD_NAME=$(kubectl get pods --namespace monitoring -l \"app.kubernetes.io/name=prometheus-node-exporter,app.kubernetes.io/instance=kube-prometheus-stack\" -o jsonpath=\"{.items[0].metadata.name}\")\n  echo \"Visit http://127.0.0.1:9100 to use your application\"\n  kubectl port-forward --namespace monitoring $POD_NAME 9100\nkube-prometheus-stack has been installed. Check its status by running:\n  kubectl --namespace monitoring get pods -l \"release=kube-prometheus-stack\"\n\nVisit https://github.com/prometheus-operator/kube-prometheus for instructions on how to create \u0026 configure Alertmanager and Prometheus instances using the Operator.\n\n1. Get your 'admin' user password by running:\n\n   kubectl get secret --namespace monitoring kube-prometheus-stack-grafana -o jsonpath=\"{.data.admin-password}\" | base64 --decode ; echo\n\n\n2. The Grafana server can be accessed via port 80 on the following DNS name from within your cluster:\n\n   kube-prometheus-stack-grafana.monitoring.svc.cluster.local\n\n   Get the Grafana URL to visit by running these commands in the same shell:\n     export POD_NAME=$(kubectl get pods --namespace monitoring -l \"app.kubernetes.io/name=grafana,app.kubernetes.io/instance=kube-prometheus-stack\" -o jsonpath=\"{.items[0].metadata.name}\")\n     kubectl --namespace monitoring port-forward $POD_NAME 3000\n\n3. Login with the password from step 1 and the username: admin\n#################################################################################\n######   WARNING: Persistence is disabled!!! You will lose your data when   #####\n######            the Grafana pod is terminated.                            #####\n#################################################################################\n\nkube-state-metrics is a simple service that listens to the Kubernetes API server and generates metrics about the state of the objects.\nThe exposed metrics can be found here:\nhttps://github.com/kubernetes/kube-state-metrics/blob/master/docs/README.md#exposed-metrics\n\nThe metrics are exported on the HTTP endpoint /metrics on the listening port.\nIn your case, kube-prometheus-stack-kube-state-metrics.monitoring.svc.cluster.local:8080/metrics\n\nThey are served either as plaintext or protobuf depending on the Accept header.\nThey are designed to be consumed either by Prometheus itself or by a scraper that is compatible with scraping a Prometheus client endpoint.\n",
                "revision": 1,
                "values": "{\"alertmanager\":{\"alertmanagerSpec\":{\"storage\":{\"emptyDir\":{}}}},\"grafana\":{\"enabled\":true,\"service\":{\"type\":\"ClusterIP\"},\"sidecar\":{\"dashboards\":{\"enabled\":true,\"folder\":\"/var/lib/grafana/dashboards/default\",\"label\":\"grafana_dashboard\"}}},\"kubeStateMetrics\":{\"enabled\":true},\"nodeExporter\":{\"enabled\":true},\"prometheus\":{\"prometheusSpec\":{\"storageSpec\":{\"emptyDir\":{}}}},\"prometheusOperator\":{\"createCustomResource\":true}}",
                "version": "59.0.0"
              }
            ],
            "name": "kube-prometheus-stack",
            "namespace": "monitoring",
            "pass_credentials": false,
            "postrender": [],
            "recreate_pods": false,
            "render_subchart_notes": true,
            "replace": false,
            "repository": "https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts",
            "repository_ca_file": null,
            "repository_cert_file": null,
            "repository_key_file": null,
            "repository_password": null,
            "repository_username": null,
            "reset_values": false,
            "reuse_values": false,
            "set": [],
            "set_list": [],
            "set_sensitive": [],
            "skip_crds": false,
            "status": "deployed",
            "timeout": 600,
            "upgrade_install": null,
            "values": [
              "#############################################################################\r\n# values/kube-prometheus-values.yaml\r\n#\r\n# We keep Grafana enabled + sidecar so dashboards auto-load. We keep\r\n# nodeExporter/kubeStateMetrics. BUT we override Prometheus/Alertmanager\r\n# to use emptyDir (so no PVCs are needed).\r\n#############################################################################\r\n\r\n################################################################################\r\n# 1) Enable the chart’s built-in Grafana sub-chart (will install Grafana in monitoring/)\r\n################################################################################\r\ngrafana:\r\n  enabled: true\r\n\r\n  # Tell the Grafana sidecar to import every ConfigMap labeled \"grafana_dashboard\"\r\n  sidecar:\r\n    dashboards:\r\n      enabled: true\r\n      label: grafana_dashboard\r\n      folder: /var/lib/grafana/dashboards/default\r\n\r\n  # We expose Grafana as ClusterIP; you will port-forward to 3000\r\n  service:\r\n    type: ClusterIP\r\n\r\n################################################################################\r\n# 2) Keep Prometheus Operator CRDs \u0026 core components active\r\n################################################################################\r\nprometheusOperator:\r\n  createCustomResource: true\r\n\r\n################################################################################\r\n# 3) Enable the Kubernetes exporters you want\r\n################################################################################\r\nnodeExporter:\r\n  enabled: true\r\n\r\nkubeStateMetrics:\r\n  enabled: true\r\n\r\n################################################################################\r\n# 4) Override Prometheus/Alertmanager to use emptyDir (no PVC needed)\r\n################################################################################\r\nprometheus:\r\n  prometheusSpec:\r\n    # storageSpec tells Prometheus to use an ephemeral directory rather than PVC\r\n    storageSpec:\r\n      emptyDir: {}\r\n\r\nalertmanager:\r\n  alertmanagerSpec:\r\n    # storage tells Alertmanager to use an ephemeral directory instead of PVC\r\n    storage:\r\n      emptyDir: {}\r\n      \r\n################################################################################\r\n# 5) Disable pieces you don’t need right now (e.g. Alertmanager’s persistent alerts,\r\n#    etc.). Since we disabled storage above, Alertmanager is effectively ephemeral.\r\n################################################################################\r\n# (We already told alertmanager.storage → emptyDir)\r\n# You can also turn off Alertmanager entirely if you like:\r\n# alertmanager:\r\n#   enabled: false\r\n"
            ],
            "verify": false,
            "version": "59.0.0",
            "wait": false,
            "wait_for_jobs": false
          },
